WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:03.760 --> 00:01:08.479
The Nobel Peace Prize gives winners a

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:11.040
huge platform and quite a lot of money.

00:01:08.479 --> 00:01:12.799
It's worth just over $1 million

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:17.040
and ironically, the whole thing was the

00:01:12.799 --> 00:01:19.439
idea of the man who invented dynamite.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.280
Alfred Nobel was a Swedish scientist and

00:01:19.439 --> 00:01:22.080
one of the wealthiest businessmen of his

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:25.439
time.

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:27.520
When he died in 1896, his will said his

00:01:25.439 --> 00:01:30.560
fortune should be used to establish a

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:33.280
foundation for five prizes in physics,

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:34.799
chemistry, medicine, literature

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.799
and peace.

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:46.720
When it comes to the Peace Prize, Alfred

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:49.119
Nobel said the winner should be the

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.439
person who has done "the most or best to

00:01:49.119 --> 00:01:53.360
advance fellowship among nations, the

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:55.280
abolition or reduction of standing

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:57.439
armies, and the establishment and

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:59.280
promotion of peace congresses".

00:01:57.439 --> 00:02:01.439
But that's been interpreted in different

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:03.520
ways over the years by the Nobel Peace

00:02:01.439 --> 00:02:06.799
Prize committee. 

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.480
The very first prize was awarded in 1901, to

00:02:30.319 --> 00:02:34.959
the Swiss humanitarian, Jean-Henri

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:38.800
Dunant and Frédéric Passy, the founder

00:02:34.959 --> 00:02:38.800
of the first French peace society.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.239
Since then there have been another 135

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:48.400
laureates. Some of the highest profile

00:02:46.239 --> 00:02:52.239
and least controversial winners include

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:54.480
Martin Luther King ...

00:02:54.480 --> 00:03:00.400
Mother Teresa, the Dalai Lama, Nelson

00:02:57.599 --> 00:03:02.159
Mandela and Malala Yousafzai. The

00:03:00.400 --> 00:03:04.400
Pakistani advocate for childhood

00:03:02.159 --> 00:03:06.180
education was also the youngest ever

00:03:04.400 --> 00:03:08.239
winner—she was 17.

00:03:08.239 --> 00:03:12.720
Organisations have sometimes won, too. 

00:03:10.480 --> 00:03:15.599
Like the UN World Food Programme, which won

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.360
last year. OK, so how are the winners

00:03:15.599 --> 00:03:20.239
chosen? Well, it starts with the

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:22.319
nominations. To be eligible to submit a

00:03:20.239 --> 00:03:24.720
nomination you have to fall into one of

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:26.480
nine categories. For example, members of

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.159
parliaments anywhere in the world,

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:31.040
university professors in certain

00:03:28.159 --> 00:03:33.040
subjects, or previous winners. And that

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:35.680
range of people who can nominate has

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:38.400
made for some pretty strange nominees,

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:39.280
among them Hitler and Stalin back in the

00:03:38.400 --> 00:03:41.280
day.

00:03:39.280 --> 00:03:43.760
And we only know that now because the

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:45.440
actual list of nominees is kept secret

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.599
for 50 years.

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.840
Then we get to this election that's done

00:03:47.599 --> 00:03:52.159
by the Norwegian Nobel Committee-a group

00:03:49.840 --> 00:03:54.239
of five people chosen by the Norwegian

00:03:52.159 --> 00:03:55.920
Parliament. And that's something that's

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:58.159
different about the Peace Prize because

00:03:55.920 --> 00:04:00.000
all the other Nobel Prizes are decided

00:03:58.159 --> 00:04:02.400
in Sweden. The Norwegian Committee

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.319
consults various advisers along the way,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.799
but in the end it comes down to a

00:04:04.319 --> 00:04:09.120
majority vote among its five members. 

00:04:27.759 --> 00:04:32.639
The thing is, to a lot of

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:34.720
people some of the committee's picks

00:04:32.639 --> 00:04:37.120
have just seemed a bit off over the

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.120
years.

00:04:37.199 --> 00:04:41.919
Henry Kissinger got it in 1973 for his

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.600
efforts to negotiate a ceasefire in the

00:04:41.919 --> 00:04:46.320
Vietnam War when he was the US

00:04:43.600 --> 00:04:48.639
secretary of state. That was despite his

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:51.360
alleged role in the US's devastating

00:04:48.639 --> 00:04:53.280
bombing campaign in neighboring Cambodia.

00:04:51.360 --> 00:04:55.199
The New York Times called it the "Nobel

00:04:53.280 --> 00:04:57.040
War Prize" and two members of the

00:04:55.199 --> 00:04:58.960
committee, who had voted against him,

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.560
resigned in protest.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:02.720
And then there have been prizes which

00:05:00.560 --> 00:05:04.320
were given as more of an encouragement,

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.080
where the committee hoped the prize

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.960
itself would further the cause of the

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.759
recipient. They've also been contentious.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:13.680
Like when the European Union won in 2012

00:05:11.759 --> 00:05:16.560
for helping to establish peace between

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:18.479
former enemies at exactly the time when

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:21.440
the eurozone financial crisis was

00:05:18.479 --> 00:05:24.240
exposing all the cracks in the union.

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.800
Obama won less than a year into his

00:05:40.479 --> 00:05:45.199
presidency. He hadn't really done much

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:47.360
yet and he was commander-in-chief of the

00:05:45.199 --> 00:05:49.520
most powerful army in the world that had

00:05:47.360 --> 00:05:51.600
thousands of soldiers in Afghanistan and

00:05:49.520 --> 00:05:53.520
Iraq.

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:08.639
And that's another thing—

00:06:06.720 --> 00:06:10.720
people sometimes question the Peace

00:06:08.639 --> 00:06:13.120
Prize because some of the winners don't

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:15.280
end up being all that peaceful. So, you

00:06:13.120 --> 00:06:18.240
can end up with a Nobel Peace laureate

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:20.720
like Ethiopian President Abiy Ahmed. He

00:06:18.240 --> 00:06:23.759
won in 2019 for brokering peace with

00:06:20.720 --> 00:06:26.080
Eritrea. But right now there's a civil

00:06:23.759 --> 00:06:28.400
war going on in Ethiopia, where there

00:06:26.080 --> 00:06:31.680
have been reports of massacres, rape and

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:33.759
torture on both sides. And Abiy is accused

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.919
of deliberately blocking the supply of

00:06:33.759 --> 00:06:37.919
food and aid to people there who are

00:06:35.919 --> 00:06:39.759
starving. 

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:47.120
Then there's Aung San Suu Kyi, who won

00:06:44.800 --> 00:06:49.520
back in 1991 for standing up for

00:06:47.120 --> 00:06:51.440
democracy in Myanmar. But since then,

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:53.759
she's been accused of failing to condemn

00:06:51.440 --> 00:06:56.400
her country's military for its role in

00:06:53.759 --> 00:06:58.479
the targeted killing and displacement of

00:06:56.400 --> 00:07:00.800
ethnic Rohingya. Now despite the

00:06:58.479 --> 00:07:03.840
criticism, the Nobel rules say the

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:05.360
committee can't take back the prize. 

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:31.039
From the Oscars to the

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.720
Pulitzers, in the end, most prizes are

00:07:31.039 --> 00:07:34.800
about congratulating someone and

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:37.280
recognising something they've done. And

00:07:34.800 --> 00:07:39.280
the Nobel Peace Prize is no different.

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:41.759
But, as well as that, it can also have

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.000
other really positive effects, like the

00:07:41.759 --> 00:07:46.639
impact of the prize money. Remember, it's

00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:48.400
worth more than $1 million. 

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:58.879
There are also the benefits

00:07:56.319 --> 00:08:01.759
that international fame and prestige can

00:07:58.879 --> 00:08:03.440
bring. 

00:08:11.360 --> 00:08:15.280
But there can also be

00:08:13.120 --> 00:08:17.199
downsides to all that attention—

00:08:15.280 --> 00:08:20.000
like the political pressure. 

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:38.880
So, let's go back to our question—

00:08:37.200 --> 00:08:39.760
what's the point of the Nobel Peace

00:08:38.880 --> 00:08:42.240
Prize?

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:45.120
Well, 120 years later we're obviously

00:08:42.240 --> 00:08:47.440
still nowhere near world peace. But even

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.760
if it falls short of that—and let's be

00:08:47.440 --> 00:08:52.399
honest, that's a pretty big ask—

00:08:49.760 --> 00:08:54.560
it's still got a lot going for it.

